# TypeScript Development Guidelines

## Type Safety Requirements
- Use TypeScript strict mode for all files
- Define proper interfaces for all component props and data structures
- Avoid using `any` type - use proper typing or `unknown` with type guards
- Implement type guards for external data validation
- Use generic types where appropriate for reusable components

## Interface and Type Definitions
- Create interfaces for all component props in the same file or dedicated types file
- Use descriptive names that clearly indicate the data structure purpose
- Export types that might be used across multiple components
- Group related types in dedicated files under `src/lib/types/`

## Component Props Typing
```typescript
interface ComponentProps {
  title: string;
  description?: string;
  isActive: boolean;
  onClick: (id: string) => void;
  children?: React.ReactNode;
}

const Component: React.FC<ComponentProps> = ({ title, description, isActive, onClick, children }) => {
  // Component implementation
};
```

## API and Data Typing
- Define interfaces for all API responses and external data
- Use proper typing for EmailJS configuration and responses
- Type all project data and mock content structures
- Implement proper error typing for async operations

## Event Handling
- Use proper event types for all event handlers
- Type custom event handlers with specific parameter types
- Implement proper keyboard event typing for accessibility features

## Three.js and Animation Typing
- Use @types/three for Three.js components
- Type all animation parameters and configuration objects
- Implement proper typing for GSAP and Framer Motion configurations
- Use proper refs typing for DOM manipulation

## Utility Function Typing
- Type all utility functions with clear input and output types
- Use function overloads when functions accept multiple parameter patterns
- Implement proper typing for configuration objects and constants# TypeScript Development Guidelines

